5. Small utilities contributed by us, labelled as ours: wall-clock logger, normalization script, checkpointing/eval harness — none exist upstream `[UNVERIFIED: expected absent, confirmed at 0.8]`.

**Primary analysis plan in one line:** for each map family and each axis (steps, wall-clock), report median curves, AUC, time-to-threshold at 0.5/0.9 with censoring, and final-window win rates with bootstrap CIs, per arm; conclusions drawn from raw win rates; normalized score and IQ-Learn attribution handled exactly as §§2 and 4 stipulate.", "evidence": "Design-only deliverable built on verified environment inspection plus researcher-approved plan; no runs performed, all repo facts flagged [UNVERIFIED] and gated behind the Step-0 checklist (Section 0 of the design). Verified in-sandbox facts: /workspace contains only task.json; find over the filesystem shows no jaxmarl/SMAX code; jax not installed; network resolution fails (no clone/pip possible); nvidia-smi fails (no GPU) — establishing the CPU-only feasibility row. Resolution arithmetic shown in Section 6 is standard binomial measurement error: SE=√(p(1−p)/n); at p=0.6, n=32 → SE≈0.087 and difference-of-means 95% half-width ≈0.108 at 5 seeds (fails a 0.10-gap test), while n=128 → half-width ≈0.053 (passes); 5-vs-5 Mann-Whitney minimum attainable p = 2/C(10,5) ≈ 0.008. Normalized-score circularity removed by freezing reference ceilings at Step 0.7 (published per-map SMAX win rates) with a declared-now fixed fallback (0.95/0.80), never an output of the compared arms; raw win rates mandated alongside every normalized value. Wall-clock accounting fixed as two quantities (total incl. compile+eval; train-only with compile reported as a separate per-arm column); eval cost quantified in an explicit ledger (20 evals × 128 episodes × horizon per 20M-step run) rather than left implicit. All four researcher tightening requests are addressed: §4 ceiling rule, §2 attribution paragraph, §5 equal-axis timing, §6 seed arithmetic + win-rate-vs-reward split.", "assumptions": ["All repository-level claims (PPOConfig/make_ff_mappo, runners/smax_runner.py, IQLearn + smax_iql_runner.py, available_configs, W&B logging, absence of timing/normalization utilities, published-run wall-clock scale) are unverified background knowledge pending the Step-0 checklist against a pinned commit — per researcher instruction.", "Execution will occur on a machine with a single modern NVIDIA GPU and JAX-CUDA; this sandbox is design-only and cannot execute even Step 0.", "No compute budget was given to the researcher; budgets (20M/50M steps, 128 eval episodes, 5 seeds) are pre-registered design choices, with the 20M/50M figures to be aligned to whatever the JaxMARL paper reports at Step 0.", "The fallback normalized-score ceiling constants (0.95 easy/medium, 0.80 hard) are declared arbitrary-but-fixed now, used only if no published reference exists for a map.", "Win flag exists in SMAX state/info (checked at Step 0.5; design halts if absent).", "Map names (1m/2m, 2s3z/3s5z, 1o_vs_10b) are family candidates only; final map set comes solely from the available_configs dump, with the stated substitution/escalation rule."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the pinned commit's IQLearn exposes a replay-buffer parameter (arm B′ in-scope vs out-of-scope hinges on Step 0.3).", "Exact SMAX map availability and per-map horizons; hard-tier coverage may be thinner than assumed.", "Published per-map reference win rates may be missing or come from PPO-family papers, which is disclosed but accepted; the declared fallback constants handle the missing case.", "Magnitude of per-step and compile-time asymmetries between arms is unknown until measured — the two wall-clock columns exist precisely to expose this.", "Between-seed variance may dominate measurement noise at final performance; the escalation rule (add seeds for final tables, keep eval size fixed) is pre-registered but the need for it is unknown.", "Whether IQ-Learn trains above random on medium/hard maps at all; a floor result is a legitimate finding the design is built to report, not prevent."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ac15635f11802132", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-ac15635f11802132", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: PPO vs. Q-Learning in JaxMARL's SMAX \u2014 Training Time and Normalized Score\n\n**Status: design only. Nothing below was run; no number below is a result.** All repo-level statements carry a `[UNVERIFIED]` tag: background knowledge of the public `flairox/jaxmarl` repo that Step 0 must confirm against a pinned commit.\n\n**Scope note in one sentence:** this design compares (A) JaxMARL's default PPO for SMAX \u2014 FF-MAPPO `[UNVERIFIED: PPOConfig/PPO/make_ff_mappo in jaxmarl/utils, runners/smax_runner.py, Hydra YAMLs under configs/]` \u2014 against (B) the repo's only Q-learning, IQ-Learn (independent deep Q-learning per agent, epsilon-greedy, target network) `[UNVERIFIED: IQLearn in jaxmarl/utils, runners/smax_iql_runner.py]`, plus (C) a random-policy floor, on three SMAX map families.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Step-0 Verification Checklist (first-class, blocking)\n\nNothing is executed until every box is ticked against a **pinned commit (SHA recorded in the pre-registration file)**. If a box fails, the noted fallback applies.\n\n| # | Item to check | How | If it fails |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.1 | Clone `flairox/jaxmarl`; record commit SHA, JAX/jaxlib versions, GPU build | `git`, `pip freeze` | \u2014 |\n| 0.2 | PPO path exists: PPO class + `make_ff_mappo`/`make_mappo` factories; runner entry point | read `jaxmarl/utils`, `runners/`, `configs/` | stop; re-pin |\n| 0.3 | IQ-Learn exists: class + runner + its Hydra config; **does its config expose a replay-buffer parameter?** | read source of the IQLearn class | see \u00a72 limitation arm B\u2032 |\n| 0.4 | Dump `available_configs` from `jaxmarl/envs/smax/smax_env.py`: exact map names, `n_agents`, `n_enemies`, obs/action dims, horizon per map | programmatic dump into pre-registration file | map families (\u00a73) resolved from the dump only \u2014 never guessed |\n| 0.5 | Win flag: confirm the SMAX state/info field that signals a win, and that the runner logs episodic win rate and reward | read env + runner code | if win absent, stop \u2014 the primary outcome is undefined |\n| 0.6 | Record default hyperparameters per arm from YAMLs (num_envs, num_steps, lr, minibatches, gamma, epsilon schedule). Divergences between the two arms' defaults are **logged, not silently unified**; we unify only: env-step budget, eval protocol, hardware | read configs | \u2014 |\n| 0.7 | Extract published per-map reference win rates from the JaxMARL (Rutherford et al. 2024) and SMAX (Lancashire et al. 2023) papers for the chosen maps; freeze into `references.yaml` **before any run** | read paper tables | use fallback reference rule, \u00a74 |\n| 0.8 | Confirm W&B logging exists; confirm no built-in wall-clock/normalized-score utilities (expected: none) | grep | we add timing ourselves regardless |\n| 0.9 | Hardware probe of the execution machine: GPU model, `jax.devices()`, memory | one-cell smoke test, excluded from analysis | if CPU-only, see \u00a77 |\n\n**Outputs of Step 0:** pinned SHA, `maps.yaml` (from 0.4), `references.yaml` (from 0.7), `hyperparams.yaml` (from 0.6). These four files + the protocol below *are* the pre-registration.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why this comparison is hard, stated up front\n\nPPO is on-policy and IQ-Learn is off-policy *in principle*, but the shipped JaxMARL IQ-Learn updates on freshly collected batches `[UNVERIFIED \u2014 confirmed at 0.3]`, i.e. with little or no data reuse. That asymmetry is a property of the available baseline and stays in the report, not under the rug. We therefore never claim \"PPO vs Q-learning under identical conditions\"; we claim \"PPO vs the repo's shipped Q-learning baseline, matched on env-step budget and hardware.\"\n\n## 2. Arms, and the \"is it IQ-Learn's fault or Q-learning's fault?\" paragraph\n\n- **A \u2014 FF-MAPPO** (repo default for SMAX), default hyperparameters, our unified budget.\n- **B \u2014 IQ-Learn** as shipped. **Stated limitation:** most readers asking about \"Q-learning in SMAC\" mean *centralized* deep Q-learning with value decomposition (QMIX-style) and a replay buffer. That is not arm B. Arm B is independent, (likely) bufferless deep Q-learning \u2014 a weaker, non-cooperative reading of the question. If the design's honest headline is \"independent bufferless deep Q-learning trails PPO,\" so be it; **we do not swap in a different baseline if B floors out.**\n- **C \u2014 Random policy**: uniform action sampling, ~1000 episodes per map, gives the normalized-score floor `WR_random` per map.\n\n**Attribution paragraph.** If B lands at or near random on medium/hard maps, a reader will ask whether the failure is IQ-Learn's (this implementation, no replay, independent training) or of Q-learning as a family. This design **cannot fully settle that, and the deliverable says so.** What it can do: **Arm B\u2032** \u2014 IQ-Learn with a modest replay buffer (~5k transitions) \u2014 included **only if** Step 0.3 shows the parameter already exists in the pinned commit (a config line, no new code). If it requires writing a buffer, B\u2032 is **explicitly out of scope** (flagged, not silently dropped). Even a successful B\u2032 only separates \"no data reuse hurts\" from \"deep independent Q fails anyway\"; separating \"independent Q fails\" from \"all Q-learning fails\" would need a centralized Q method (QMIX etc.) absent from the repo, which is out of scope, full stop.\n\n## 3. Maps (families, resolved only from the 0.4 dump)\n\n| Family | Named candidates (in priority order) | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| easy-small | `1m`, then `2m` | near-solvable sanity + ceiling behaviour |\n| medium-heterogeneous | `2s3z`, then `3s5z` | where algorithms should separate |\n| hard | `1o_vs_10b`, then the largest heterogeneous map in `available_configs` | stress / floor behaviour |\n\n**Substitution rule:** take the first candidate present in the pinned `available_configs`; if none in a family is present, escalate rather than improvise a fourth option. SMAC's easy/medium/hard/super-hard tiers are only partially representable in SMAX `[UNVERIFIED]`; we report which tiers are actually covered, not SMAC's labels. No agent counts or obs/action sizes are stated anywhere in this design until 0.4 fills them.\n\n## 4. Outcome 2: normalized score (fixed to remove circularity)\n\n**One-sentence definition, in the deliverable:** *Normalized score = (an algorithm's evaluated win rate \u2212 the random policy's win rate on the same map) \u00f7 (a pre-registered reference win rate \u2212 the random policy's win rate), clipped to [0, 1].*\n\n- **Ceiling, primary:** per-map published SMAX win rates extracted at Step 0.7 and frozen in `references.yaml` before any run.\n- **Ceiling, fallback (declared now, used only if no published number exists for a map):** a fixed constant \u2014 **0.95** for easy/medium families, **0.80** for hard \u2014 explicitly labelled \"declared arbitrary-but-fixed reference,\" never derived from any run in this experiment.\n- **Anti-circularity rule:** the ceiling is never an output of the compared arms. PPO's own run is not the reference under any branch. (If 0.7 yields published ceilings *from PPO papers*, that is disclosed but accepted as the field's standard reference; the fallback constant is the alternative.)\n- **Hard reporting rule:** raw win rate with 95% seed-bootstrap CIs is **always** reported alongside; every headline claim cites raw win rate first; the normalized value is a convenience rescaling and no conclusion may rest on it alone.\n- **Metric source = win rate.** SMAX emits both a dense reward and a win flag; thresholds (\u00a76) and normalized score use **win rate only**. Reward is plotted as a **secondary trace** (per-checkpoint mean episodic reward with inter-seed band), because early learning in Q-learners is often visible in shaped reward long before wins appear.\n\n*Illustrative arithmetic only, not a result:* if WR_random = 0.02 (measured, arm C), frozen reference = 0.90, and PPO reaches 0.66 win rate, normalized score = (0.66\u22120.02)/(0.90\u22120.02) = 0.73.\n\n## 5. Outcome 1: training time (two axes as equals, no single headline ranking)\n\nThree recorded quantities per run:\n1. **Environment steps to threshold** \u2014 the fairness-correct compute axis.\n2. **Wall-clock to threshold on identical hardware** \u2014 the practitioner axis.\n3. **Fixed-budget totals**: steps/second and total wall-clock at budget end.\n\n**Wall-clock accounting, fixed now:**\n- `wall_clock_total` = XLA/JIT compilation + training + evaluation. Every arm pays for its own eval and compile inside this number.\n- `wall_clock_train` = same, excluding first-call compile time and excluding eval intervals; compile time is additionally reported **per arm, as its own column** (PPO's compiled graph is plausibly heavier `[UNVERIFIED]`; whether that flips rankings is exactly what the two columns expose).\n- **No headline ranking claim is made on either axis.** They are reported side by side, and the deliverable states that wall-clock rankings are specific to the logged hardware and are meaningless on CPU-only (\u00a77). What could flip the two axes' orderings is enumerated as a report section: per-step update cost asymmetry, compile-time share (large only for short runs), vectorization/throughput differences, and eval-overhead share.\n\n**Eval cost ledger (not free-floating):** evals run every 1M training steps; 128 episodes \u00d7 map horizon H (H read at 0.4) per eval; at a 20M-step budget that is 20 evals \u00d7 128 \u00d7 H env steps and its own wall-clock. These are **excluded from both step and `wall_clock_train` budgets** (same exclusion for both arms, so symmetric) but **reported per run in an appendix ledger** so nobody has to trust that evals were cheap.\n\n## 6. Evaluation, seeds and resolution arithmetic (no asserted 5, derived 5)\n\n**Protocol:** eval checkpoint every 1M env steps; greedy/deterministic policy for both arms (pre-registered); win rate = fraction won of 128 episodes; thresholds at 0.5 and 0.9; budgets pre-registered at 20M steps (easy/medium) and 50M (hard), adjusted at Step 0 to whatever the JaxMARL paper reports for its SMAX runs if different.\n\n**Resolution, with arithmetic.** Question: can we tell a true win rate of 0.55 from 0.65? Per checkpoint, measured win rate \u2248 Binomial(p, n)/n, SE = \u221a(p(1\u2212p)/n). At p = 0.6:\n- n = 32: SE \u2248 0.087 \u2192 two-algorithm difference-of-means SE at s=5 seeds = SE\u00b7\u221a(2/s) = 0.055 \u2192 95% half-width \u2248 0.108. **Underpowered** for a 0.10 gap from measurement noise alone.\n- n = 128: SE \u2248 0.043 \u2192 same chain \u2192 half-width \u2248 0.053. **Passes.** n = 256 \u2192 0.038; not worth doubling cost.\n- Checkpoint threshold-crossing detection: at n=128, CI half-width at p\u22480.9 is 1.96\u00b7\u221a(0.09/128) \u2248 0.05 \u2014 crossing 0.9 is detectable within one checkpoint.\n\n**Decision: 5 seeds, 128 eval episodes** (measurement noise is the cheap knob; we turn it). Seeds stay at 5 because the seed-to-seed *variance* is signal we want to report, not noise to average away: per-seed final-window win rates and full curves are always shown; final performance = win rate over the last 10% of steps. Cross-seed comparisons use percentile bootstrap over seeds and Mann\u2013Whitney U with exact p noted as low-powered at 5v5 (smallest attainable p \u2248 0.008). **Honest caveat:** if between-seed spread is large (final win rates clustering near 0 or 1 \u2014 common in SMAX), the binding constraint is seeds, not eval episodes; the pre-registered escalation rule is then: add seeds for final tables only, never change eval size mid-experiment.\n\n**Learning-curve analysis:** median curve + inter-seed quartile band; AUC over log-env-steps with bootstrap CI; time-to-threshold tables with censoring (\"never crossed within budget\" reported as such, not dropped); secondary reward trace per \u00a74.\n\n## 7. Feasibility (including the only hardware actually present)\n\n| Hardware | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| 1\u00d7 modern NVIDIA GPU (A100/4090-class), JAX-CUDA \u2014 **assumed** execution target | Feasible. SMAX PPO runs are reported as minutes-to-~1-hour-scale at these budgets `[UNVERIFIED paper/README claims]`; grid = 3 maps \u00d7 {A, B, C} \u00d7 5 seeds (+B\u2032 if armed) \u2248 30\u201345 runs \u2248 hours to ~1 day GPU. |\n| **This sandbox: CPU-only (224 cores), no GPU, no network, no jax, no repo** | **Not an execution host.** Cannot clone (Step 0 impossible); JAX-CPU SMAX throughput makes hard maps infeasible and easy-map runs days-long; any wall-clock ranking produced here is meaningless \u2014 only the steps-axis would be interpretable, and we would not trust it as the comparison. |\n| Multi-GPU | Not needed; adds cross-arm contention risk for no benefit. |\n\n## 8. Deliverable\n\n1. Pre-registration bundle: pinned SHA, `maps.yaml`, `references.yaml`, `hyperparams.yaml`, this protocol.\n2. Per-map plots: win-rate vs env-steps **and** vs wall-clock (both arms as equals, random floor line, quartile bands); secondary reward traces.\n3. Tables: raw win rates + CIs (primary), time-to-threshold per axis (censored values shown), normalized score (secondary, always next to raw), compile-time column, eval-overhead ledger.\n4. **\"Known unfairnesses\" note:** bufferless independent Q \u2260 what SMAC readers mean by Q-learning (\u00a72); per-step cost asymmetry; hardware-specific wall-clock; reference-ceiling choice; default-hyperparameter divergences kept as shipped (\u00a70.6).\n5. Small utilities contributed by us, labelled as ours: wall-clock logger, normalization script, checkpointing/eval harness \u2014 none exist upstream `[UNVERIFIED: expected absent, confirmed at 0.8]`.\n\n**Primary analysis plan in one line:** for each map family and each axis (steps, wall-clock), report median curves, AUC, time-to-threshold at 0.5/0.9 with censoring, and final-window win rates with bootstrap CIs, per arm; conclusions drawn from raw win rates; normalized score and IQ-Learn attribution handled exactly as \u00a7\u00a72 and 4 stipulate.", "evidence": "Design-only deliverable built on verified environment inspection plus researcher-approved plan; no runs performed, all repo facts flagged [UNVERIFIED] and gated behind the Step-0 checklist (Section 0 of the design). Verified in-sandbox facts: /workspace contains only task.json; find over the filesystem shows no jaxmarl/SMAX code; jax not installed; network resolution fails (no clone/pip possible); nvidia-smi fails (no GPU) \u2014 establishing the CPU-only feasibility row. Resolution arithmetic shown in Section 6 is standard binomial measurement error: SE=\u221a(p(1\u2212p)/n); at p=0.6, n=32 \u2192 SE\u22480.087 and difference-of-means 95% half-width \u22480.108 at 5 seeds (fails a 0.10-gap test), while n=128 \u2192 half-width \u22480.053 (passes); 5-vs-5 Mann-Whitney minimum attainable p = 2/C(10,5) \u2248 0.008. Normalized-score circularity removed by freezing reference ceilings at Step 0.7 (published per-map SMAX win rates) with a declared-now fixed fallback (0.95/0.80), never an output of the compared arms; raw win rates mandated alongside every normalized value. Wall-clock accounting fixed as two quantities (total incl. compile+eval; train-only with compile reported as a separate per-arm column); eval cost quantified in an explicit ledger (20 evals \u00d7 128 episodes \u00d7 horizon per 20M-step run) rather than left implicit. All four researcher tightening requests are addressed: \u00a74 ceiling rule, \u00a72 attribution paragraph, \u00a75 equal-axis timing, \u00a76 seed arithmetic + win-rate-vs-reward split.", "assumptions": ["All repository-level claims (PPOConfig/make_ff_mappo, runners/smax_runner.py, IQLearn + smax_iql_runner.py, available_configs, W&B logging, absence of timing/normalization utilities, published-run wall-clock scale) are unverified background knowledge pending the Step-0 checklist against a pinned commit \u2014 per researcher instruction.", "Execution will occur on a machine with a single modern NVIDIA GPU and JAX-CUDA; this sandbox is design-only and cannot execute even Step 0.", "No compute budget was given to the researcher; budgets (20M/50M steps, 128 eval episodes, 5 seeds) are pre-registered design choices, with the 20M/50M figures to be aligned to whatever the JaxMARL paper reports at Step 0.", "The fallback normalized-score ceiling constants (0.95 easy/medium, 0.80 hard) are declared arbitrary-but-fixed now, used only if no published reference exists for a map.", "Win flag exists in SMAX state/info (checked at Step 0.5; design halts if absent).", "Map names (1m/2m, 2s3z/3s5z, 1o_vs_10b) are family candidates only; final map set comes solely from the available_configs dump, with the stated substitution/escalation rule."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the pinned commit's IQLearn exposes a replay-buffer parameter (arm B\u2032 in-scope vs out-of-scope hinges on Step 0.3).", "Exact SMAX map availability and per-map horizons; hard-tier coverage may be thinner than assumed.", "Published per-map reference win rates may be missing or come from PPO-family papers, which is disclosed but accepted; the declared fallback constants handle the missing case.", "Magnitude of per-step and compile-time asymmetries between arms is unknown until measured \u2014 the two wall-clock columns exist precisely to expose this.", "Between-seed variance may dominate measurement noise at final performance; the escalation rule (add seeds for final tables, keep eval size fixed) is pre-registered but the need for it is unknown.", "Whether IQ-Learn trains above random on medium/hard maps at all; a floor result is a legitimate finding the design is built to report, not prevent."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-af231e42-4703-4755-97e7-627041557360", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: PPO vs. Q-Learning in JaxMARL's SMAX \u2014 Training Time and Normalized Score\n\n**Status: design only. Nothing below was run; no number below is a result.** All repo-level statements carry a `[UNVERIFIED]` tag: background knowledge of the public `flairox/jaxmarl` repo that Step 0 must confirm against a pinned commit.\n\n**Scope note in one sentence:** this design compares (A) JaxMARL's default PPO for SMAX \u2014 FF-MAPPO `[UNVERIFIED: PPOConfig/PPO/make_ff_mappo in jaxmarl/utils, runners/smax_runner.py, Hydra YAMLs under configs/]` \u2014 against (B) the repo's only Q-learning, IQ-Learn (independent deep Q-learning per agent, epsilon-greedy, target network) `[UNVERIFIED: IQLearn in jaxmarl/utils, runners/smax_iql_runner.py]`, plus (C) a random-policy floor, on three SMAX map families.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Step-0 Verification Checklist (first-class, blocking)\n\nNothing is executed until every box is ticked against a **pinned commit (SHA recorded in the pre-registration file)**. If a box fails, the noted fallback applies.\n\n| # | Item to check | How | If it fails |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.1 | Clone `flairox/jaxmarl`; record commit SHA, JAX/jaxlib versions, GPU build | `git`, `pip freeze` | \u2014 |\n| 0.2 | PPO path exists: PPO class + `make_ff_mappo`/`make_mappo` factories; runner entry point | read `jaxmarl/utils`, `runners/`, `configs/` | stop; re-pin |\n| 0.3 | IQ-Learn exists: class + runner + its Hydra config; **does its config expose a replay-buffer parameter?** | read source of the IQLearn class | see \u00a72 limitation arm B\u2032 |\n| 0.4 | Dump `available_configs` from `jaxmarl/envs/smax/smax_env.py`: exact map names, `n_agents`, `n_enemies`, obs/action dims, horizon per map | programmatic dump into pre-registration file | map families (\u00a73) resolved from the dump only \u2014 never guessed |\n| 0.5 | Win flag: confirm the SMAX state/info field that signals a win, and that the runner logs episodic win rate and reward | read env + runner code | if win absent, stop \u2014 the primary outcome is undefined |\n| 0.6 | Record default hyperparameters per arm from YAMLs (num_envs, num_steps, lr, minibatches, gamma, epsilon schedule). Divergences between the two arms' defaults are **logged, not silently unified**; we unify only: env-step budget, eval protocol, hardware | read configs | \u2014 |\n| 0.7 | Extract published per-map reference win rates from the JaxMARL (Rutherford et al. 2024) and SMAX (Lancashire et al. 2023) papers for the chosen maps; freeze into `references.yaml` **before any run** | read paper tables | use fallback reference rule, \u00a74 |\n| 0.8 | Confirm W&B logging exists; confirm no built-in wall-clock/normalized-score utilities (expected: none) | grep | we add timing ourselves regardless |\n| 0.9 | Hardware probe of the execution machine: GPU model, `jax.devices()`, memory | one-cell smoke test, excluded from analysis | if CPU-only, see \u00a77 |\n\n**Outputs of Step 0:** pinned SHA, `maps.yaml` (from 0.4), `references.yaml` (from 0.7), `hyperparams.yaml` (from 0.6). These four files + the protocol below *are* the pre-registration.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why this comparison is hard, stated up front\n\nPPO is on-policy and IQ-Learn is off-policy *in principle*, but the shipped JaxMARL IQ-Learn updates on freshly collected batches `[UNVERIFIED \u2014 confirmed at 0.3]`, i.e. with little or no data reuse. That asymmetry is a property of the available baseline and stays in the report, not under the rug. We therefore never claim \"PPO vs Q-learning under identical conditions\"; we claim \"PPO vs the repo's shipped Q-learning baseline, matched on env-step budget and hardware.\"\n\n## 2. Arms, and the \"is it IQ-Learn's fault or Q-learning's fault?\" paragraph\n\n- **A \u2014 FF-MAPPO** (repo default for SMAX), default hyperparameters, our unified budget.\n- **B \u2014 IQ-Learn** as shipped. **Stated limitation:** most readers asking about \"Q-learning in SMAC\" mean *centralized* deep Q-learning with value decomposition (QMIX-style) and a replay buffer. That is not arm B. Arm B is independent, (likely) bufferless deep Q-learning \u2014 a weaker, non-cooperative reading of the question. If the design's honest headline is \"independent bufferless deep Q-learning trails PPO,\" so be it; **we do not swap in a different baseline if B floors out.**\n- **C \u2014 Random policy**: uniform action sampling, ~1000 episodes per map, gives the normalized-score floor `WR_random` per map.\n\n**Attribution paragraph.** If B lands at or near random on medium/hard maps, a reader will ask whether the failure is IQ-Learn's (this implementation, no replay, independent training) or of Q-learning as a family. This design **cannot fully settle that, and the deliverable says so.** What it can do: **Arm B\u2032** \u2014 IQ-Learn with a modest replay buffer (~5k transitions) \u2014 included **only if** Step 0.3 shows the parameter already exists in the pinned commit (a config line, no new code). If it requires writing a buffer, B\u2032 is **explicitly out of scope** (flagged, not silently dropped). Even a successful B\u2032 only separates \"no data reuse hurts\" from \"deep independent Q fails anyway\"; separating \"independent Q fails\" from \"all Q-learning fails\" would need a centralized Q method (QMIX etc.) absent from the repo, which is out of scope, full stop.\n\n## 3. Maps (families, resolved only from the 0.4 dump)\n\n| Family | Named candidates (in priority order) | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| easy-small | `1m`, then `2m` | near-solvable sanity + ceiling behaviour |\n| medium-heterogeneous | `2s3z`, then `3s5z` | where algorithms should separate |\n| hard | `1o_vs_10b`, then the largest heterogeneous map in `available_configs` | stress / floor behaviour |\n\n**Substitution rule:** take the first candidate present in the pinned `available_configs`; if none in a family is present, escalate rather than improvise a fourth option. SMAC's easy/medium/hard/super-hard tiers are only partially representable in SMAX `[UNVERIFIED]`; we report which tiers are actually covered, not SMAC's labels. No agent counts or obs/action sizes are stated anywhere in this design until 0.4 fills them.\n\n## 4. Outcome 2: normalized score (fixed to remove circularity)\n\n**One-sentence definition, in the deliverable:** *Normalized score = (an algorithm's evaluated win rate \u2212 the random policy's win rate on the same map) \u00f7 (a pre-registered reference win rate \u2212 the random policy's win rate), clipped to [0, 1].*\n\n- **Ceiling, primary:** per-map published SMAX win rates extracted at Step 0.7 and frozen in `references.yaml` before any run.\n- **Ceiling, fallback (declared now, used only if no published number exists for a map):** a fixed constant \u2014 **0.95** for easy/medium families, **0.80** for hard \u2014 explicitly labelled \"declared arbitrary-but-fixed reference,\" never derived from any run in this experiment.\n- **Anti-circularity rule:** the ceiling is never an output of the compared arms. PPO's own run is not the reference under any branch. (If 0.7 yields published ceilings *from PPO papers*, that is disclosed but accepted as the field's standard reference; the fallback constant is the alternative.)\n- **Hard reporting rule:** raw win rate with 95% seed-bootstrap CIs is **always** reported alongside; every headline claim cites raw win rate first; the normalized value is a convenience rescaling and no conclusion may rest on it alone.\n- **Metric source = win rate.** SMAX emits both a dense reward and a win flag; thresholds (\u00a76) and normalized score use **win rate only**. Reward is plotted as a **secondary trace** (per-checkpoint mean episodic reward with inter-seed band), because early learning in Q-learners is often visible in shaped reward long before wins appear.\n\n*Illustrative arithmetic only, not a result:* if WR_random = 0.02 (measured, arm C), frozen reference = 0.90, and PPO reaches 0.66 win rate, normalized score = (0.66\u22120.02)/(0.90\u22120.02) = 0.73.\n\n## 5. Outcome 1: training time (two axes as equals, no single headline ranking)\n\nThree recorded quantities per run:\n1. **Environment steps to threshold** \u2014 the fairness-correct compute axis.\n2. **Wall-clock to threshold on identical hardware** \u2014 the practitioner axis.\n3. **Fixed-budget totals**: steps/second and total wall-clock at budget end.\n\n**Wall-clock accounting, fixed now:**\n- `wall_clock_total` = XLA/JIT compilation + training + evaluation. Every arm pays for its own eval and compile inside this number.\n- `wall_clock_train` = same, excluding first-call compile time and excluding eval intervals; compile time is additionally reported **per arm, as its own column** (PPO's compiled graph is plausibly heavier `[UNVERIFIED]`; whether that flips rankings is exactly what the two columns expose).\n- **No headline ranking claim is made on either axis.** They are reported side by side, and the deliverable states that wall-clock rankings are specific to the logged hardware and are meaningless on CPU-only (\u00a77). What could flip the two axes' orderings is enumerated as a report section: per-step update cost asymmetry, compile-time share (large only for short runs), vectorization/throughput differences, and eval-overhead share.\n\n**Eval cost ledger (not free-floating):** evals run every 1M training steps; 128 episodes \u00d7 map horizon H (H read at 0.4) per eval; at a 20M-step budget that is 20 evals \u00d7 128 \u00d7 H env steps and its own wall-clock. These are **excluded from both step and `wall_clock_train` budgets** (same exclusion for both arms, so symmetric) but **reported per run in an appendix ledger** so nobody has to trust that evals were cheap.\n\n## 6. Evaluation, seeds and resolution arithmetic (no asserted 5, derived 5)\n\n**Protocol:** eval checkpoint every 1M env steps; greedy/deterministic policy for both arms (pre-registered); win rate = fraction won of 128 episodes; thresholds at 0.5 and 0.9; budgets pre-registered at 20M steps (easy/medium) and 50M (hard), adjusted at Step 0 to whatever the JaxMARL paper reports for its SMAX runs if different.\n\n**Resolution, with arithmetic.** Question: can we tell a true win rate of 0.55 from 0.65? Per checkpoint, measured win rate \u2248 Binomial(p, n)/n, SE = \u221a(p(1\u2212p)/n). At p = 0.6:\n- n = 32: SE \u2248 0.087 \u2192 two-algorithm difference-of-means SE at s=5 seeds = SE\u00b7\u221a(2/s) = 0.055 \u2192 95% half-width \u2248 0.108. **Underpowered** for a 0.10 gap from measurement noise alone.\n- n = 128: SE \u2248 0.043 \u2192 same chain \u2192 half-width \u2248 0.053. **Passes.** n = 256 \u2192 0.038; not worth doubling cost.\n- Checkpoint threshold-crossing detection: at n=128, CI half-width at p\u22480.9 is 1.96\u00b7\u221a(0.09/128) \u2248 0.05 \u2014 crossing 0.9 is detectable within one checkpoint.\n\n**Decision: 5 seeds, 128 eval episodes** (measurement noise is the cheap knob; we turn it). Seeds stay at 5 because the seed-to-seed *variance* is signal we want to report, not noise to average away: per-seed final-window win rates and full curves are always shown; final performance = win rate over the last 10% of steps. Cross-seed comparisons use percentile bootstrap over seeds and Mann\u2013Whitney U with exact p noted as low-powered at 5v5 (smallest attainable p \u2248 0.008). **Honest caveat:** if between-seed spread is large (final win rates clustering near 0 or 1 \u2014 common in SMAX), the binding constraint is seeds, not eval episodes; the pre-registered escalation rule is then: add seeds for final tables only, never change eval size mid-experiment.\n\n**Learning-curve analysis:** median curve + inter-seed quartile band; AUC over log-env-steps with bootstrap CI; time-to-threshold tables with censoring (\"never crossed within budget\" reported as such, not dropped); secondary reward trace per \u00a74.\n\n## 7. Feasibility (including the only hardware actually present)\n\n| Hardware | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| 1\u00d7 modern NVIDIA GPU (A100/4090-class), JAX-CUDA \u2014 **assumed** execution target | Feasible. SMAX PPO runs are reported as minutes-to-~1-hour-scale at these budgets `[UNVERIFIED paper/README claims]`; grid = 3 maps \u00d7 {A, B, C} \u00d7 5 seeds (+B\u2032 if armed) \u2248 30\u201345 runs \u2248 hours to ~1 day GPU. |\n| **This sandbox: CPU-only (224 cores), no GPU, no network, no jax, no repo** | **Not an execution host.** Cannot clone (Step 0 impossible); JAX-CPU SMAX throughput makes hard maps infeasible and easy-map runs days-long; any wall-clock ranking produced here is meaningless \u2014 only the steps-axis would be interpretable, and we would not trust it as the comparison. |\n| Multi-GPU | Not needed; adds cross-arm contention risk for no benefit. |\n\n## 8. Deliverable\n\n1. Pre-registration bundle: pinned SHA, `maps.yaml`, `references.yaml`, `hyperparams.yaml`, this protocol.\n2. Per-map plots: win-rate vs env-steps **and** vs wall-clock (both arms as equals, random floor line, quartile bands); secondary reward traces.\n3. Tables: raw win rates + CIs (primary), time-to-threshold per axis (censored values shown), normalized score (secondary, always next to raw), compile-time column, eval-overhead ledger.\n4. **\"Known unfairnesses\" note:** bufferless independent Q \u2260 what SMAC readers mean by Q-learning (\u00a72); per-step cost asymmetry; hardware-specific wall-clock; reference-ceiling choice; default-hyperparameter divergences kept as shipped (\u00a70.6).\n5. Small utilities contributed by us, labelled as ours: wall-clock logger, normalization script, checkpointing/eval harness \u2014 none exist upstream `[UNVERIFIED: expected absent, confirmed at 0.8]`.\n\n**Primary analysis plan in one line:** for each map family and each axis (steps, wall-clock), report median curves, AUC, time-to-threshold at 0.5/0.9 with censoring, and final-window win rates with bootstrap CIs, per arm; conclusions drawn from raw win rates; normalized score and IQ-Learn attribution handled exactly as \u00a7\u00a72 and 4 stipulate.", "evidence": "Design-only deliverable built on verified environment inspection plus researcher-approved plan; no runs performed, all repo facts flagged [UNVERIFIED] and gated behind the Step-0 checklist (Section 0 of the design). Verified in-sandbox facts: /workspace contains only task.json; find over the filesystem shows no jaxmarl/SMAX code; jax not installed; network resolution fails (no clone/pip possible); nvidia-smi fails (no GPU) \u2014 establishing the CPU-only feasibility row. Resolution arithmetic shown in Section 6 is standard binomial measurement error: SE=\u221a(p(1\u2212p)/n); at p=0.6, n=32 \u2192 SE\u22480.087 and difference-of-means 95% half-width \u22480.108 at 5 seeds (fails a 0.10-gap test), while n=128 \u2192 half-width \u22480.053 (passes); 5-vs-5 Mann-Whitney minimum attainable p = 2/C(10,5) \u2248 0.008. Normalized-score circularity removed by freezing reference ceilings at Step 0.7 (published per-map SMAX win rates) with a declared-now fixed fallback (0.95/0.80), never an output of the compared arms; raw win rates mandated alongside every normalized value. Wall-clock accounting fixed as two quantities (total incl. compile+eval; train-only with compile reported as a separate per-arm column); eval cost quantified in an explicit ledger (20 evals \u00d7 128 episodes \u00d7 horizon per 20M-step run) rather than left implicit. All four researcher tightening requests are addressed: \u00a74 ceiling rule, \u00a72 attribution paragraph, \u00a75 equal-axis timing, \u00a76 seed arithmetic + win-rate-vs-reward split.", "assumptions": ["All repository-level claims (PPOConfig/make_ff_mappo, runners/smax_runner.py, IQLearn + smax_iql_runner.py, available_configs, W&B logging, absence of timing/normalization utilities, published-run wall-clock scale) are unverified background knowledge pending the Step-0 checklist against a pinned commit \u2014 per researcher instruction.", "Execution will occur on a machine with a single modern NVIDIA GPU and JAX-CUDA; this sandbox is design-only and cannot execute even Step 0.", "No compute budget was given to the researcher; budgets (20M/50M steps, 128 eval episodes, 5 seeds) are pre-registered design choices, with the 20M/50M figures to be aligned to whatever the JaxMARL paper reports at Step 0.", "The fallback normalized-score ceiling constants (0.95 easy/medium, 0.80 hard) are declared arbitrary-but-fixed now, used only if no published reference exists for a map.", "Win flag exists in SMAX state/info (checked at Step 0.5; design halts if absent).", "Map names (1m/2m, 2s3z/3s5z, 1o_vs_10b) are family candidates only; final map set comes solely from the available_configs dump, with the stated substitution/escalation rule."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the pinned commit's IQLearn exposes a replay-buffer parameter (arm B\u2032 in-scope vs out-of-scope hinges on Step 0.3).", "Exact SMAX map availability and per-map horizons; hard-tier coverage may be thinner than assumed.", "Published per-map reference win rates may be missing or come from PPO-family papers, which is disclosed but accepted; the declared fallback constants handle the missing case.", "Magnitude of per-step and compile-time asymmetries between arms is unknown until measured \u2014 the two wall-clock columns exist precisely to expose this.", "Between-seed variance may dominate measurement noise at final performance; the escalation rule (add seeds for final tables, keep eval size fixed) is pre-registered but the need for it is unknown.", "Whether IQ-Learn trains above random on medium/hard maps at all; a floor result is a legitimate finding the design is built to report, not prevent."]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
